<main>
<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">Discourse Graphs · Claude Code plugin</p>
  <h1>dgflow: get from ticket to a PR you can trust</h1>
  <p class="lede">dgflow is a set of Claude Code commands that walk a Linear ticket all the way to a reviewer-ready pull request. You make the decisions. The agent does the busywork and shows you <strong>proof</strong> that the change works.</p>
  <p class="meta">Version 0.1.0 · private to the DiscourseGraphs org · <a href="https://github.com/DiscourseGraphs/dgflow">github.com/DiscourseGraphs/dgflow</a></p>
</header>

<section id="why">
  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>AI agents write code fast. The hard part is <em>trusting</em> it. Did it actually work in the app? Did it change things nobody asked for? Will reviewers find problems the agent should have caught? Usually you end up re-checking everything by hand.</p>
  <p>dgflow takes the opposite approach. You agree on a plan with the agent up front. The agent then has to <strong>prove</strong> its work with tests and screenshots of the real app, before you ever look at the PR. You only step in at two moments: approving the plan, and asking for review.</p>
</section>

<section id="flow">
  <h2>How it flows</h2>
  <p>Here's what one ticket looks like from start to finish. Orange boxes are yours. Purple boxes are where the agent stops and asks you. Everything else runs by itself.</p>
  <div class="diagram"><pre class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    A["Pick a ticket&lt;br/&gt;/dgflow:start ENG-1234"] --> B["Talk through a plan with the agent"]
    B --> G1{{"You approve the plan"}}
    G1 --> C["Agent saves the plan&lt;br/&gt;/dgflow:checkpoint"]
    C --> D["Agent writes the code"]
    D --> E["Agent tests it: unit tests,&lt;br/&gt;or clicks through the real app"]
    E -->|"something broke"| D
    E --> F["Agent saves screenshots as proof&lt;br/&gt;dgflow:pr-evidence"]
    F --> P["Agent checks and reviews its own work,&lt;br/&gt;then opens the PR&lt;br/&gt;/dgflow:create-pr"]
    P -->|"did more than the plan said"| Q["Agent asks you first"]
    Q --> P
    P --> R["Agent answers review bots and fixes CI&lt;br/&gt;dgflow:babysit"]
    R -->|"spots a repeat mistake"| L["Agent suggests a rule&lt;br/&gt;so it never happens again"]
    L --> R
    R --> G2{{"You look at the proof,&lt;br/&gt;record a Loom, ask for review"}}
    classDef you fill:#FFF1E8,stroke:#C9561C,color:#1F1F1F,stroke-width:2px
    classDef ask fill:#F0EFFA,stroke:#5F57C0,color:#1F1F1F
    class G1,G2 you
    class Q,L ask
  </pre></div>
  <div class="legend"><span class="lg-you">You decide</span><span class="lg-ask">Agent asks you</span><span class="lg-agent">Agent does it</span></div>

  <p style="margin-top:20px">The same flow as steps:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><div><b>Start the ticket.</b><small><code>/dgflow:start ENG-1234</code> reads the ticket, makes a separate copy of the repo for it (a "worktree"), installs dependencies, and starts the app.</small></div></li>
    <li class="you"><div><b>Plan together, then approve.</b><small>Talk it through. Say "approved, ship when verified" if you want the agent to open the PR on its own once everything passes.</small></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Agent builds and proves it.</b><small>It writes the code, then tests it the cheapest way that actually proves it works: a unit test for logic, or driving the real Roam or Obsidian app for UI. Screenshots get saved as proof.</small></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Agent opens a clean PR.</b><small><code>/dgflow:create-pr</code> runs the checks, has a fresh agent review the diff, compares the result against your plan, and fills the PR template with a diagram and the proof.</small></div></li>
    <li><div><b>Agent handles the bots.</b><small><code>babysit</code> answers Devin, Codex and Graphite and fixes CI until everything is green.</small></div></li>
    <li class="you"><div><b>You check and send it.</b><small>Look at the proof, record a short Loom, and request a review.</small></div></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="pick">
  <h2>Pick what you need</h2>
  <p>You don't have to use the whole flow. Installing dgflow gives you every command, but you can use only the ones that help.</p>
  <div class="recipes">
    <div class="recipe"><q>"The agent forgets everything between sessions."</q><p>Use <code>/dgflow:checkpoint</code> at the end of a session, and <code>/dgflow:go-ticket</code> to pick up where you left off.</p></div>
    <div class="recipe"><q>"My PRs keep getting the same review comments."</q><p>Use <code>/dgflow:create-pr</code> to open PRs and <code>babysit</code> to handle bot feedback.</p></div>
    <div class="recipe"><q>"I'm never sure it actually works."</q><p>Use <code>dg-obsidian-cdp-verify</code> for Obsidian, the team's Roam loader for Roam, and <code>pr-evidence</code> to save screenshots.</p></div>
    <div class="recipe"><q>"I juggle several tickets at once."</q><p>Use <code>/dgflow:start</code>, <code>go-ticket</code> and <code>cleanup-ticket</code>. Each ticket gets its own copy of the repo.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="pieces">
  <h2>Every piece</h2>
  <p>Badges: <span class="badge b-proven">proven</span> used on many tickets · <span class="badge b-caveats">caveats</span> works, with known rough edges · <span class="badge b-exp">experimental</span> new, expect changes.</p>
  <div class="cards">
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:setup</span><span class="badge b-exp">experimental</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Checks your tools and asks for your personal settings, one question at a time.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> So nothing in the plugin is hardcoded to one person's computer.</p>
      <p class="needs">Run once after installing. Run again to change a setting.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/commands/setup.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:start</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Turns a Linear ticket into a ready-to-code workspace: branch, worktree, dependencies, running app.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Removes ten minutes of setup per ticket, and keeps tickets from interfering with each other.</p>
      <p class="needs">Needs Linear connected in Claude Code.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/commands/start.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:checkpoint</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Writes the goal, decisions, approved plan and next step for this branch into one markdown file.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Agent sessions forget everything when they end. A checkpoint saves the reasoning so the next session, or a teammate, picks up where you left off.</p>
      <p class="needs">Saves to a folder you choose (an Obsidian vault works too).</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/checkpoint/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:go-ticket</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Jumps back into a ticket's workspace and loads its checkpoint.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Resume in seconds, without re-explaining anything.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/commands/go-ticket.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:create-pr</span><span class="badge b-caveats">caveats</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Checks and reviews the work, compares it against the plan, and opens a PR with a diagram and proof.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Reviewers get a PR that's easy to trust and quick to read. No more fixing the PR body by hand.</p>
      <p class="needs">Uses four team skills from the discourse-graph repo; setup links them. The PR-watching step only works in the Claude desktop app.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/create-pr/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">dgflow:babysit</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Answers review bots, fixes CI and resolves simple conflicts until the PR is green.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> The back-and-forth with bots stops being your job. Repeat mistakes become rules, with your OK.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/babysit/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">dgflow:pr-evidence</span><span class="badge b-caveats">caveats</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Uploads test screenshots to a page in your Roam dev graph and returns links for the PR.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Proof travels with the PR, organised by branch and test run.</p>
      <p class="needs">Needs a signed-in Roam Playwright slot. GitHub shows the images as links, not inline.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/pr-evidence/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">dgflow:dg-obsidian-cdp-verify</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Builds your branch into a test vault, clicks through the real Obsidian app, and screenshots each test case.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> "Types pass" isn't proof. This shows it working in the app.</p>
      <p class="needs">macOS and the Obsidian app. It may restart Obsidian to turn on remote control.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/dg-obsidian-cdp-verify/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">dgflow:obsidian-vault</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Lends each branch its own Obsidian test vault.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Two tickets can't overwrite each other's plugin build mid-test. Your production vault is never touched.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/obsidian-vault/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:cleanup-ticket</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Removes a merged ticket's worktree and branch, plus its vault lease.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Keeps your disk and branch list tidy.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/commands/cleanup-ticket.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">/dgflow:roam-switch</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Points your Roam dev extension at a different ticket's build.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Test another branch in Roam without rebuilding links by hand.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/commands/roam-switch.md">View file</a></article>
    <article class="card"><header><span class="name">dgflow:explain-diff-html</span><span class="badge b-proven">proven</span></header>
      <p class="row"><b>What:</b> Explains a code change as an interactive web page, with a quiz.</p>
      <p class="row"><b>Why:</b> Actually understand what the agent wrote. Great for learning.</p>
      <a class="src" href="$G/skills/explain-diff-html/SKILL.md">View file</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="install">
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>Type these into Claude Code one at a time.</p>
  <div class="install">
    <div class="cmd"><span class="n">1</span><div><code id="c1">/plugin marketplace add anthropics/claude-plugins-official</code><small>Usually already set up; running it again is harmless. dgflow needs it to install its companion skills.</small></div><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="c1">Copy</button></div>
    <div class="cmd"><span class="n">2</span><div><code id="c2">/plugin marketplace add DiscourseGraphs/dgflow</code><small>Tells Claude Code where dgflow lives. Uses your GitHub access, since the repo is private to the org.</small></div><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="c2">Copy</button></div>
    <div class="cmd"><span class="n">3</span><div><code id="c3">/plugin install dgflow@dgflow</code><small>Installs dgflow and mattpocock-skills together. Restart Claude Code afterwards.</small></div><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="c3">Copy</button></div>
    <div class="cmd"><span class="n">4</span><div><code id="c4">/dgflow:setup</code><small>A guided chat: checks your tools and asks for your folders, Roam slot and Obsidian test vaults. Skip anything you don't use.</small></div><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="c4">Copy</button></div>
  </div>
  <p>Then try it: <code>/dgflow:start ENG-1234</code>, using a real ticket number. To update later: <code>/plugin marketplace update dgflow</code>.</p>
  <div class="callout warn"><strong>If <code>/plugin list</code> says dgflow "failed to load"</strong> because of a missing dependency, run step 1, then step 3 again.</div>
</section>

<section id="companions">
  <h2>Companion skills (installed automatically)</h2>
  <p>dgflow pulls in Matt Pocock's skill pack. These are the ones that fit this flow best:</p>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:grill-me</code></td><td>You have a plan and want it stress-tested with tough questions before any code gets written.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:grilling</code></td><td>Same idea, run as rounds of numbered questions with recommended answers. Good for bigger designs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:teach</code></td><td>You want to actually learn a concept in this codebase, not just get it done.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:tdd</code></td><td>You're building logic and want tests written first, one small step at a time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:prototype</code></td><td>You're unsure about a design and want a throwaway version to try first.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs</code></td><td>Something is broken and you want a careful debugging loop instead of guesses.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/mattpocock-skills:handoff</code></td><td>You're stopping and want a summary another session or teammate can pick up.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<section id="example">
  <h2>What the proof looks like</h2>
  <p>Every screenshot is filed under its branch and test run in Roam, so you can always trace it back to the exact code that produced it.</p>
  <figure class="shot">
    <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64,$B64" alt="A Roam page holding branch blocks, each with dated test runs whose children are captioned screenshots, including an Obsidian window showing a link inserted at the cursor" width="1200" height="1152" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>One branch per block, then one run per date and commit, then one captioned screenshot per test case. This run checked that "insert link" puts the link exactly at the cursor in Obsidian.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="glossary">
  <h2>Glossary</h2>
  <dl class="gloss">
    <dt>Command / skill</dt><dd>A saved set of instructions Claude Code follows. You run a command by typing it, like <code>/dgflow:start</code>. Skills can also kick in on their own when they fit.</dd>
    <dt>Plugin</dt><dd>A bundle of commands and skills you install in one step.</dd>
    <dt>Worktree</dt><dd>A second copy of the repo on its own branch, so each ticket has a clean, separate workspace.</dd>
    <dt>Checkpoint</dt><dd>A markdown note that saves what was decided and what's next, so work survives between sessions.</dd>
    <dt>CI</dt><dd>The automatic checks GitHub runs on every PR: type checks, tests, formatting.</dd>
    <dt>Review bots</dt><dd>Devin, Codex and Graphite. They comment on PRs automatically. Codex and Graphite skip draft PRs, so dgflow opens PRs as ready.</dd>
    <dt>PR template</dt><dd>The required headings in every PR description. dgflow reads the template fresh each time, so it never goes out of date.</dd>
    <dt>Scenario</dt><dd>One test case the agent runs in the real app, like "type a link, check it lands at the cursor".</dd>
    <dt>CDP</dt><dd>Chrome DevTools Protocol, a way for a script to drive an app like Obsidian, which is built on Chrome, as if someone were clicking.</dd>
    <dt>Gate</dt><dd>A moment where a person has to decide before the agent continues.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section id="faq">
  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Does it cost a lot of tokens?</b></td><td>About 700 tokens are added to every session so Claude knows the commands exist. Each command costs more only when you use it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>I use Codex.</b></td><td>babysit, explain-diff-html and obsidian-vault work as-is. Most others work partly: the instructions apply, but the hooks and desktop-only steps don't.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Windows or Linux?</b></td><td>The Obsidian verification is macOS-only. Most other pieces work anywhere Git and Node run.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The team skills aren't found.</b></td><td>The discourse-graph repo doesn't commit its <code>.claude/skills</code> links yet, so a fresh clone can't see them. Run <code>/dgflow:setup</code>, which offers to link them for you.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Can I change something?</b></td><td>Yes. Commit straight to <a href="https://github.com/DiscourseGraphs/dgflow">the repo</a>, add a line to CHANGELOG.md tagged proven / caveats / experimental, and bump the version. Questions go to the engineering Slack channel.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

